#
# SilkXotic v1.2.0 "Habotai" — khungtaskd, log-only (freeze forensics).
#
# Builds the hung-task detector in: any task blocked in uninterruptible (D) state for
# more than 120 s gets its kernel stack printed to dmesg, which klogd forwards into
# the persistent logcat. Intended so a freeze self-documents even when adb is dead —
# the 07-30 wedge left us with no stack for the blocked task, only the symptom.
#
# Log-only by design — BOOTPARAM_HUNG_TASK_PANIC stays off, so a false positive (a
# slow I/O stall, a suspended task) never costs the user a reboot.
#
# !! THIS CONFIG ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH — verified on-device 2026-09-04. AOSP's
#    /system/etc/init/hw/init.rc writes "hung_task_timeout_secs 0" a few seconds into
#    every boot, which disables the detector regardless of the compiled default below.
#    (Android's intended replacement, llkd, is also inert here: ro.llk.enable and
#    ro.khungtask.* are all unset on crDroid and ro.debuggable=0, so llkd.rc never
#    re-arms it either.) Same override class as the scheduler knobs — see ROADMAP.
#
#    To actually arm it, something must write the sysctl AFTER init:
#        echo 120 > /proc/sys/kernel/hung_task_timeout_secs
#    A KSU module doing that at boot is the cheapest option. Keeping this fragment is
#    still correct: the detector must be compiled in before anything can enable it.
#
# Depends only on DEBUG_KERNEL=y, already on in this build (silkxotic-slim.config
# keeps it as the gate SCHED_DEBUG needs). Overhead is one task-list scan per 120 s,
# and none at all while the timeout is 0.
#
CONFIG_DETECT_HUNG_TASK=y
CONFIG_DEFAULT_HUNG_TASK_TIMEOUT=120
# CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_HUNG_TASK_PANIC is not set

#
# --- Added 2026-09-07 after freezes #5 and #6 ---
#
# The hung-task detector above is necessary but NOT sufficient, and freeze #6 proved it:
# with hung_task_panic=1 and panic_on_rcu_stall=1 both armed and verified live, the phone
# stayed wedged for 13 minutes (6x the 120 s threshold) without ever panicking. A panic
# resets via CONFIG_QCOM_FORCE_WDOG_BITE_ON_PANIC and does not need printk to work, so
# khungtaskd genuinely scanned and found nothing.
#
#   => no task was in TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE >120 s, and there was no RCU stall.
#
# CONFIG_QCOM_WATCHDOG_V2=y was also live and never bit, so it was not a hard lockup
# either. What that leaves on the kernel side is a CPU spinning in-kernel: such a task
# stays in state R and is invisible to khungtaskd by construction.
#
# SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR is the detector for exactly that case (>20 s in kernel mode without
# yielding, tunable via watchdog_thresh). Panic is left OFF at build time on purpose --
# armed at runtime instead from the KSU module's service.sh, both so one build can A/B it
# and because on this device the compiled default has been overridden by userspace three
# times now. Verify /proc/sys/kernel/softlockup_panic at runtime, never the config.
#
# WQ_WATCHDOG directly tests the leading reading of the kernel-log silence: most of this
# device's kernel output is charger polling driven from workqueues, so "the kernel went
# quiet" and "the workqueues stalled" predict the same observation. It prints
# "BUG: workqueue lockup" plus a workqueue state dump after 30 s of no forward progress
# (workqueue.watchdog_thresh). Log-only -- there is no panic variant -- but it reaches us
# through the pstore console and the independent /dev/kmsg reader added to service.sh.
#
# HARDLOCKUP_DETECTOR is deliberately absent: arm64 has no HAVE_PERF_EVENTS_NMI in this
# tree, so the perf-based detector cannot be built. QCOM_WATCHDOG_V2 already covers it.
#
# Both depend only on DEBUG_KERNEL=y, already on via silkxotic-slim.config.
#
CONFIG_SOFTLOCKUP_DETECTOR=y
# CONFIG_BOOTPARAM_SOFTLOCKUP_PANIC is not set
CONFIG_WQ_WATCHDOG=y
